config apn_autoconfig_esim 'main'
	option state_dir '/var/run/apn-autoconfig-esim'
	option lock_root '/var/lock/apn-autoconfig-esim'
	option action_state_dir '/tmp/apn-autoconfig-esim-action'
	# How long a read of an eUICC may be reused. Sixty seconds is chosen
	# against the only thing that can invalidate an entry without this package
	# seeing it: a change the operator pushed over RSP. Everything this package
	# performs invalidates the entry itself, immediately and by name. Accepted
	# only within 0 and 900; a value outside that range is a configuration
	# error and the command fails rather than running with an unbounded cache.
	# Zero disables reuse without disabling the cache's bookkeeping.
	option esim_cache_ttl_seconds '60'
	# The outer bound on a consented ModemManager inhibit, and the number the
	# plan quotes as the worst case. It is a watchdog, not a schedule: the
	# inhibit lasts as long as the process holding it, and this is how long that
	# process may live before it is terminated and the modem returned.
	#
	# Four minutes, against a measured worst case of roughly eighty seconds.
	#
	# The number moved twice and both moves are worth knowing. It was 180 while
	# a download was one card transaction; when the download grew a notification
	# delivery it needed 434 s, the watchdog released the modem at 180 on the dot
	# and the operation carried on against a modem ModemManager had taken back.
	# It went to 600 to stop that. Then the cost work found what the 434 s
	# actually were -- a control-port probe borrowing the card budget, repeated
	# per LPA invocation -- and the same download became 58 s, a read takeover
	# 23 s, and an interrupted load's asking-again at most twenty seconds more.
	#
	# So this is a bound with about three times the margin over what has been
	# measured, and not a schedule. Overstating it makes the plan promise more
	# disruption than usually occurs and invites a user to conclude the router
	# has hung; understating it once meant using a card this package no longer
	# owned. A download additionally refuses to start unless another uplink is
	# proven, so the outage is never the router's only path out.
	option esim_inhibit_max_seconds '240'
	# Per-exchange bound on the AT transport under the bridge. One APDU is a
	# single command and a single reply; a card that has not answered in this
	# long is not going to.
	option apdu_timeout_seconds '10'
	# Bound on asking a port whether it is the control channel, which is a much
	# smaller question than an exchange with a card and used to borrow the
	# budget above. Measured on the reference router: a live port answers `AT`
	# in under a second, a silent one costs the whole bound twice over, and four
	# of that modem's six ports are silent. Too short a value fails closed -- the
	# port is skipped, and a bridge with no answering candidate refuses rather
	# than opening a channel it has not proven.
	option at_probe_timeout_seconds '3'
	# How long an interrupted profile load waits for the card to finish before
	# it concludes that nothing happened. Zero, because on the reference card
	# waiting here cannot work.
	#
	# The measurement: the final APDU of every Bound Profile Package load on
	# this modem is refused with a bare ERROR after three seconds, and the eUICC
	# installs the profile anyway. Twenty seconds of asking found nothing;
	# ninety seconds -- twelve looks -- also found nothing; and the very next
	# operation, after the inhibit was released and ModemManager re-enumerated
	# the modem, found the profile and both of its notifications. Twice.
	#
	# So the card finishes when it is handed back and re-initialised, not while
	# this operation holds it, and a wait that cannot succeed is pure outage.
	# The knob stays because this is one card's behaviour and another may
	# differ; raise it only with a measurement that shows waiting helps there.
	option install_settle_seconds '0'
	# The whole of one LPA invocation, including every APDU exchange inside it.
	option lpa_timeout_seconds '120'
	# HTTPS to an SM-DP+, on behalf of the LPA. The persistent configuration has
	# no downgrade switch: the exceptional unverified retry is accepted only as
	# an explicit input to one download and is never written here.
	option http_timeout_seconds '60'
	# How long to wait for a lock before refusing. Contention is normal and
	# brief: another component of this suite is reading identity over the same
	# port, or the AT-dial protocol handler is holding it for a bring-up.
	# Waiting is what keeps an ordinary overlap from surfacing as a failure.
	# Zero refuses immediately. Failing to acquire is always a refusal and
	# never a licence to use the port anyway.
	option lock_wait_seconds '15'
	# How long a `delete-plan` token stays claimable, and the only knob the
	# guarded deletion has. Long enough for a person to read a confirmation and
	# type four digits; short enough that the state the plan bound -- the
	# attachment generation, the slot, the owner, the profile's own state --
	# cannot plausibly have changed unobserved while the token sat there.
	#
	# Accepted only within 30 and 600, and rejected rather than clamped: a
	# token that lives ten times as long as the contract allows is a capability
	# to destroy a subscription lying around for an afternoon, and running
	# anyway would be obeying a configuration that says that is fine.
	option delete_plan_ttl_seconds '120'
